{"name":"Red Trillium","hex":"#c44d4f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"red trillium","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Trillium erectum, native to eastern, northeastern North America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/red_trillium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Trillium erectum","blocks":[{"text":"Trillium erectum, the red trillium, also known as wake robin, purple trillium, bethroot, or stinking benjamin, is a species of flowering plant in the family Melanthiaceae. The plant takes its common name \"wake robin\" by analogy with the European robin, which has a red breast heralding spring. Likewise Trillium erectum is a spring ephemeral plant whose life-cycle is synchronized with that of the forests in which it lives. It is native to the eastern United States and eastern Canada from northern Georgia to Quebec and New Brunswick."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Trillium_erectum_-_Stephen%27s_Gulch_CA.jpg/330px-Trillium_erectum_-_Stephen%27s_Gulch_CA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trillium_erectum","wikidata":"Q3539177"},{"title":"Trillium","blocks":[{"text":"Trillium is a genus of about fifty flowering plant species in the family Melanthiaceae. Trillium species are native to temperate regions of North America and Asia, with the greatest diversity of species found in the southern Appalachian Mountains in the southeastern United States."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/TrilliumErectum.jpg/330px-TrilliumErectum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trillium","wikidata":"Q475629"},{"title":"Red","blocks":[{"text":"Red is the color at the long wavelength end of the visible spectrum of light, next to orange and opposite violet. It has a dominant wavelength of approximately 625–750 nanometers. It is a primary color in the RGB color model and a secondary color in the CMYK color model, and is the complementary color of cyan. Reds range from the brilliant yellow-tinged scarlet and vermillion to bluish-red crimson, and vary in shade from the pale red pink to the dark red burgundy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg/330px-Delicious_fresh_strawberries_%284735910403%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Red","wikidata":"Q3142"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg/500px-Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg/1280px-Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg/500px-Red_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red trillium close up, showing detail of ovary and stamens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_Trillium_Close_Up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Trillium_erectum_comparing_red_and_white_color_varieties.jpg/500px-Trillium_erectum_comparing_red_and_white_color_varieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Trillium_erectum_comparing_red_and_white_color_varieties.jpg/500px-Trillium_erectum_comparing_red_and_white_color_varieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Composite image showing both red and white color morphs of the flower of Trillium erectum. Note that both morphs have a dark purple gynoecium with co